#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073590 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073904 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b09eb59-3ad5-5cde-8503-3c28c9e5c546" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Multiple Transformations to a Point Using Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "6b45326c-ce13-5d2f-84df-b3698b46ea68" :question/math-academy-id "e-2888" :question/problem "The point $Q(4,3)$ is shifted $2$ units down, reflected across the $y$-axis, and then shifted $1$ unit to the right. What is the resulting point?" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the functional representations of our three transformations:\n\n- A shift of $2$ units down can be represented by the function\n$(x,y) \\mapsto (x,y-2)$.\n- A reflection in the $y$-axis can be represented by the function\n$(x,y) \\mapsto (-x,y)$.\n\n- A shift of $1$ unit to the right can be represented by the function\n$(x,y) \\mapsto (x+1,y)$.\n\nLet's apply the given transformations to the point $Q$ using our functions:\n\nFirst, we apply the shift. The image of the point\n\n$$\n\\left(4,3\\right)\n$$\n\nunder the shift is\n\n$$\n(4,3) \\mapsto (4,3-2)=(4, 1)\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the reflection. The image of the point\n\n$$\n\\left(4,1\\right)\n$$\n\nunder the reflection is\n\n$$\n(4, 1) \\mapsto(-4,1)\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the second shift. The image of the point\n\n$$\n\\left(-4,1\\right)\n$$\n\nunder the shift is\n\n$$\n(-4,1) \\mapsto (-4+1,1)=(-3, 1)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resulting point is $(-3,1)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073591 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073905 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4308331-fea0-51e1-b1c9-a3e8e122e09e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Parameters Given a Point and Its Image Under Multiple Transformations"}] :question/id #uuid "b45dae26-7996-5544-9884-d604bac62993" :question/math-academy-id "e-2893" :question/problem "The point $P(5,14)$ is reflected across the line $y=x$ and then stretched according to the function $(x,y) \\mapsto (x,ky)$. If the image of $P$ under this sequence of transformations is $(14,25)$, then what is the value of $k$?" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the given transformations.\n\nA reflection across the line $y=x$ can be represented by\n$(x,y) \\mapsto (y, x)$.\nApplying this to the point $P$, we get\n$(5,14) \\mapsto (14,5)$.\n\nNow, applying the stretch to $(14,5)$, we get\n$(14, 5) \\mapsto (14, 5k)$.\n\nSince the resulting point is $(14,25)$, by equating the $y$-coordinates, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5k = 25⟹k &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=5$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073648 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9322724c-215a-5a91-a478-e91abe4038da" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of a Polygon Under the Action of Multiple Transformations"}] :question/id #uuid "14fc5fcd-4aaa-5c46-a991-4703ceb0434c" :question/math-academy-id "e-7747" :question/problem "![](images/70/707fabf08d90a67f39a89c27b1bb12f475447d63a86ef0867fbae1fd0d80eaf9.png)\n\nThe triangle shown above is rotated by $90^{\\circ}$ counterclockwise about the origin and then reflected across the $x$-axis. What is the resulting triangle?" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the given transformation.\n\n- Rotating the triangle by $90^{\\circ}$ counterclockwise about the origin gives the following:\n\n![](images/fa/fae3cc7c804b24b6d6c35a5910963377683c3249e4b3c8f1440cc2338265ddd7.png)\n\n- Next, we take the last result and reflect it across the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/ee/ee29eebc26c9a852b5f58cc0f544c8c713f0cdae043cff519d3974639c8cb48a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073650 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1614303a-a067-5879-9ba6-2e7505109136" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of a Point Given a Sequence of Transformations"}] :question/id #uuid "0f05c408-7ea2-58d2-a8e3-663f819747d1" :question/math-academy-id "e-7793" :question/problem "The point $P$ below is rotated by $45^\\circ$ counterclockwise around the origin and then reflected across the $x$-axis. Which of the given points is the image of $P$ under this sequence of transformations?\n\n![](images/eb/eb4416a7d6a0d131ad6b535571001b61a50152c59459bf125f42dff2788411cd.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the given transformations.\n\n- Rotating the point $P$ by $45^\\circ$ counterclockwise around the origin gives the point $A$, as shown below:\n\n![](images/53/53128ffb3404a6fb27b096896f744b57e143ec2c5d630ae7ef9d80c256bfb43c.png)\n\n- Reflecting the point $A$ in the $x$-axis gives the point $D$, as shown below:\n\n![](images/23/234d8273aea8cfc59810b44f575887de0effe09d7ab95657b4d8b76baf822a04.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $D$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073905 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4308331-fea0-51e1-b1c9-a3e8e122e09e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Parameters Given a Point and Its Image Under Multiple Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7aea80f-6189-5af2-ad5c-553fcd0b7648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071331} {:answer/id #uuid "d041f878-cc2e-5e8d-99cd-7ed031b8f58d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071332} {:answer/id #uuid "e4c07830-a0f8-565b-b930-84aaacab6872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071333} {:answer/id #uuid "1fa7056b-65cd-5aa8-a4e9-c2084ebb4fbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071334} {:answer/id #uuid "397ccfcd-63d3-5ec2-a7c8-86def1a900b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071335}] :an